  "preamble": "The diphthong [au] is reduced to the monophthong [a] by two different routes. In West Greenlandic, Proto-Inuit vowel sequences coalesced into long vowels, so *au became [aː] (ataaseq 'one' beside Inuktitut atausiq), and East Greenlandic shares the outcome. In Latin, /au̯/ dissimilated to /a/ when the next syllable contained /u/: nonstandard agustus for augustus (attested from the 1st century CE) and ascultō for auscultō (2nd century CE) are the bases of Italian and Spanish agosto and Italian ascoltare. In Albanian the Proto-Albanian diphthong *au lost its second element (*dauša > dash 'ram').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Monophthongization, Compensatory Lengthening, Dissimilation",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Greenlandic (from Proto-Inuit)",
      "languageFamily": "Eskimo-Aleut",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "*atauciq 'one'",
          "to": "ataaseq",
          "note": "cf. Inuktitut atausiq; East Greenlandic (Tunumiisut) alaasiq also has aa"
        },
        {
          "from": "*uqauciq 'speech'",
          "to": "oqaaseq",
          "note": "cf. Inuktitut uqausiq; East Greenlandic oraasiq"
        },
